The [[2010 United States House of Representatives elections]] were held on November 2, 2010, with early voting taking place in some states in the weeks preceding that date. Voters chose representatives from all 435 [[List of United States congressional districts|congressional districts]] across each of the 50 [[U.S. state]]s. [[Delegate (United States Congress)|Non-voting delegates]] from the [[District of Columbia]] and four of the five inhabited [[Insular area|U.S. territories]]{{efn|Not including the [[Resident Commissioner of Puerto Rico]], who serves a four-year term.}} were also elected. These [[2010 United States elections|midterm elections]] took place nearly halfway through the first term of [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] President [[Barack Obama]]. The winners served in the [[112th United States Congress]], with seats [[United States congressional apportionment|apportioned]] among the states based on the [[2000 United States census]]. On Election Day, Democrats had held a House majority since January 2007 as a result of the [[2006 United States House of Representatives elections|2006 elections]].

== Predictions on overall outcome ==
[[File:House Ratings Data Wiki.svg|thumb]]
These were predictions of the outcome of the 2010 United States House of Representatives elections.
* [[RealClearPolitics]].
** As of November 2, 2010, RCP projected the Republicans would take 224 seats, the Democrats would take 167, and 44 races were toss-ups.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.realclearpolitics.com/epolls/2010/house/2010_elections_house_map.html | title=Battle For The House | publisher=Realclearpolitics.com | access-date=November 4, 2010}}</ref>
* [[Nate Silver]], FiveThirtyEight (New York Times)
** As of November 2, 2010, Nate Silver's prediction model projected the Republicans would win (on average) 232.2 seats, and the Democrats would win 202.8.<ref>{{cite news | last=Silver | first=Nate | url=http://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2010/10/31/the-ultimate-hour-by-hour-district-by-district-election-guide/#more-2869 | title=The Ultimate Hour-by-Hour, District-by-District Election Guide | publisher=Fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com | date= October 31, 2010 | access-date=November 4, 2010}}</ref>
* [[Patrick Ishmael]], Hot Air.com
** Ishmael predicted on October 31, 2010, that Republicans would win a net of 63 seats, +/- 3 seats.<ref>{{cite web | first=Patrick | last=Ishmael | url=http://hotair.com/greenroom/archives/2010/10/31/game-time-final-house-rankings-and-printable-cheat-sheet | title=Game Time: Final House rankings and printable cheat sheet | work=[[Hot Air (news site)|Hot Air]] | date=October 31, 2010 | access-date=November 24, 2010 | archive-date=December 3, 2010 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101203150242/http://hotair.com/greenroom/archives/2010/10/31/game-time-final-house-rankings-and-printable-cheat-sheet/ | url-status=dead }}</ref> Rasmussen Reports cited Ishmael's calls in its election preview.<ref>{{cite web | title=What To Look For On Election Night | url=http://www.rasmussenreports.com/public_content/politics/general_politics/october_2010/what_to_look_for_on_election_night | work=[[Rasmussen Reports]] | date=November 2, 2010 | access-date=November 24, 2010|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101104233038/http://www.rasmussenreports.com/public_content/politics/general_politics/october_2010/what_to_look_for_on_election_night |archive-date=November 4, 2010}}</ref> The week before, Ishmael predicted a net Republican pickup of 62–65 seats.<ref>{{cite web | first=Patrick | last=Ishmael | title=Eight days out, GOP still poised to gain 60+ House seats | url=http://hotair.com/greenroom/archives/2010/10/25/eight-days-out-gop-still-poised-to-gain-60-house-seats | work=Hot Air | date=October 25, 2010 | access-date=November 24, 2010 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101129060014/http://hotair.com/greenroom/archives/2010/10/25/eight-days-out-gop-still-poised-to-gain-60-house-seats/ | archive-date=November 29, 2010 | url-status=dead | df=mdy-all }}</ref>
* [[Sabato's Crystal Ball|Crystal Ball]]
** As of November 1, 2010, Larry Sabato predicted, "If the election were held today: + 55 Republican House seats".<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.centerforpolitics.org/crystalball/articles/category/2010-house/ | title=2010 House Ratings | publisher=Centerforpolitics.org | date=November 1, 2010 | access-date=November 4, 2010 | archive-date=October 23, 2010 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101023041747/http://www.centerforpolitics.org/crystalball/articles/category/2010-house/ | url-status=dead }}</ref>
* [[Charlie Cook]]
** On October 26, 2010, The Cook Political Report raised its House forecast to "a Democratic net loss of 48 to 60 seats, with higher losses possible."<ref>{{cite web| author=Cook Political Report| url=http://hotlineoncall.nationaljournal.com/archives/2010/10/cook-political-8.php| title=Cook Political Ups Projected Democratic Losses To 48 to 60| date=October 26, 2010| access-date=October 26, 2010| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101029154911/http://hotlineoncall.nationaljournal.com/archives/2010/10/cook-political-8.php| archive-date=October 29, 2010| url-status=dead| df=mdy-all}}</ref>
** In a February 2010 interview with ''[[National Journal]]'', he said that "it's very hard to come up with a scenario where Democrats don't lose the House. It's very hard."<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.outsidethebeltway.com/archives/democrats_will_lose_the_house_charlie_cook | title=Democrats Will Lose the House: Charlie Cook | publisher=Outsidethebeltway.com | date=February 22, 2010 | access-date=June 14, 2010}}</ref>
* [[Rasmussen Reports]]
** On November 1, 2010, Scott Rasmussen predicted the Democrats "will likely lose 55 or more seats in the House."<ref>{{cite news | author= Scott Rasmussen | url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB10001424052748703708404575586063725870380 | title=A Vote Against Dems, Not for the GOP | publisher=wsj.com | date=November 1, 2010 | access-date=November 4, 2010}}</ref>
* [[Rothenberg Political Report]].
** On October 28, 2010, Rothenberg Political Report predicted "Likely Republican gain of 55–65 seats, with gains at or above 70 seats possible."<ref>{{cite web | url=http://rothenbergpoliticalreport.com/ratings/house/october-28-2010-house-ratings | title=House Ratings | publisher=rothenbergpoliticalreport.com | date=October 28, 2010 | access-date=November 4, 2010}}</ref>
** In April 2010, [[Stuart Rothenberg]] wrote on his [[blog]] that "...the atmospherics remain strongly behind the GOP, and major Republican House gains are extremely likely" and that "it's clear that the battleground is almost entirely on Democratic soil. Obviously, control of the House is at risk."<ref>{{cite web | last=Rothenberg | first=The | url=http://rothenbergpoliticalreport.blogspot.com/2010/04/new-print-edition-house-overview.html | title=The Rothenberg Political Report: New Print Edition: House Overview | publisher=Rothenbergpoliticalreport.blogspot.com | date=April 14, 2010 | access-date=June 14, 2010}}</ref>
* [[Congressional Quarterly]].
** In October 2010, Congressional Quarterly projected the Democrats would take 195 seats, the Republicans 199, and they considered 41 races too close to call.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.cqpolitics.com/wmspage.cfm?docID=ratings-house| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101028234534/http://www.cqpolitics.com/wmspage.cfm?docID=ratings-house|archive-date=October 28, 2010 | title=CQ Politics {{pipe}} House Race Ratings Map for 2010 | publisher=Innovation.cqpolitics.com}}</ref>
** In July 2010, Congressional Quarterly projected the Democrats would take 205 seats, the Republicans 190, and they considered 40 races too close to call.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://innovation.cqpolitics.com/atlas/house2010_rr | title=CQ Politics {{pipe}} House Race Ratings Map for 2010 | publisher=Innovation.cqpolitics.com | access-date=July 11, 2010 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100710191959/http://innovation.cqpolitics.com/atlas/house2010_rr | archive-date=July 10, 2010 | url-status=dead | df=mdy-all }}</ref>

==Election ratings==
Several sites and individuals publish ratings of competitive seats. The seats listed below were considered competitive (not "safe" or "solid") by at least one of the rating groups. These ratings are based upon factors such as the strength of the incumbent (if the incumbent is running for re-election), the strength of the candidates, and the partisan history of the district (the [[Cook Partisan Voting Index]] is one example of this metric). Each rating describes the likelihood of a given outcome in the election.

Most election ratings use:
* '''Tossup''': no advantage
* '''Tilt''' (sometimes used): slight advantage
* '''Lean''': clear advantage
* '''Likely''': strong, but not certain advantage
* '''Safe''': outcome is nearly certain

The following table contains the final ratings of the competitiveness of selected races according to noted political analysts. Races which were considered safe for the incumbent's party are not included. Incumbents who did not run for re-election have parentheses around their name.

